  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 AM EDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\n- One more sunny day\n\nHigh pressure over Michigan will drift east today and that will\nhelp high clouds to the west to move overhead later this\nafternoon. Another mostly sunny day is anticipated, once the\nmorning fog burns off by mid morning. While much of the fog this\nmorning will be shallow in low lying areas and river valleys, some\nof may be dense in spots prior to burning off.\n\nnormal high temperatures are around 70, but we'll see highs in \nthe mid to upper 70s today. \n\n- Showers, isolated thunder, Wed-Fri\n\nHigh confidence that we'll see light to moderate rain Wednesday\ninto Friday. GFS and ECMWF are and have been in pretty good\nagreement since last week in showing a cold front move east across\nsouthern Canada into Lower MI and then stall out, as a southern\nstream low develops in New Mexico and moves northeast. This low\nwill not only draw Gulf moisture northward, it'll also be\nentrained by moisture from the tropical system that moved across\nBaja CA. We anticipate this to be a slow moving system, because\nthe frontal boundary will become more or less parallel to the\nupper flow and the frontal boundary over the cwa will act as a\nconduit for a lot of the moisture. Instability is pretty low which\nmake thunder chance around 15 percent or so. Thus, we're looking\nat mostly a light to occasionally moderate rainfall. QPF from\nWednesday to Thursday is roughly 1-2 inches, highest over the\nwestern cwa. \n\n- Cooler weekend, frost/freeze potential\n\nThe cold front will move through the cwa Friday, essentially\nending the rainfall. H8 temperatures fall from 16c on Thursday to\n6c by Saturday morning . That means the highs in the lower to mid\n70s early in the week will be closer to 60 over the weekend. If\nskies can clear at all, we'll be looking at lows in the mid 30s\nover the northern cwa Friday night and Saturday night and lower\n30s along US-10 Monday night; frost and freeze headlines may be\nneeded then.",
